#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125421} :activity/id #uuid "95082e0a-322a-5587-9f1a-1280f50a5124" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125419 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125790 :tutorial/content "One useful property of diagonalization is that it allows us to easily determine the power of a matrix.\n\nAs an example, we consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix ![](images/53/53b7a9efd9f2ae1ef4adc7cee5f86f784722777b8c29f268dbd0e822ff1354f9.svg) with the diagonalization\n![](images/e3/e3111a91b80d238cf5255eb9a9884dcd3f80e914039c5e789e32c07b55cf5b06.svg)\n\nWe will use the diagonalization of $A$ to compute $A^{55}.$\n\nLet's start by computing $A^2.$ Using the properties of matrix multiplication, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =PD{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =PD\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P(DD){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $A^3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot A \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P({D}^{2}D){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{3}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix $P$ and its inverse $P^{-1}$ will always cancel each other out in the middle of the multiplication! So by induction, we have that\n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}55}=P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}55} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we only need to find $D^{\\color{blue}55}.$\n\nSince $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {D}^{3} & ={D}^{2}D=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing this pattern, we get\n\n![](images/05/05e10fdc6944077a48b0214a41fae8d418a1b66c8d87a47d652158c84285c246.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{55} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{55}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given that $A = PDP^{-1},$ for any non-negative integer ${\\color{blue}k},$ we obtain \n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}k} = P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}k} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b7628bf0-02d4-514e-a564-669610253cd2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3576 :tutorial/title "Raising a Diagonalizable Matrix to a Power"} :step/id #uuid "934d1edd-bf1c-5c1f-8924-ede352d7d23b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125420}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125420 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a"} :step/id #uuid "d76255af-54d9-5dcb-9a9c-7b30b6c4a98a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125422}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125421 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125791 :tutorial/content "Remember that the diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ and corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ is\n![](images/cd/cde63e04857414720bed0cb247e1e0c4bae76b69823f4aeb494cda13c139ac13.svg)\n\nThis idea also works in reverse. Given a diagonalization $A$ in the form \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe can immediately deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are the diagonal entries of $D,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of $P.$ This is because the diagonalization of a matrix is (almost) unique.\n\n**Note:** The diagonalization is \"almost\" unique because the order of the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs may vary, and the eigenvectors may be replaced with scalar multiples of themselves. However, none of these variations change the actual outcome regarding the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs that we deduce." :tutorial/id #uuid "d23e5c1b-9483-574b-ab9f-f68b126993cd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3577 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6ed440e4-0b21-56bd-92c6-0ef75f28a68c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125422 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5"} :step/id #uuid "476486fe-26df-57a3-89ab-80eeb17483b7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e"} :step/id #uuid "1265d446-04a7-5d76-a5b8-e7c56bb1b4c0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125419}}] :activity/title "Properties of Diagonalization" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124754} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492090} :activity/id #uuid "3a218002-61a4-554f-ad7b-1fff0bdc3316" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354492 :question/id #uuid "18c9e3a0-322a-5b85-8d72-26e41db87e30"} :step/id #uuid "1ba7f498-1881-5ab1-ad8c-dead16debd6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492103}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323905 :question/id #uuid "55fe01d2-18d3-58de-b08f-22bbfece1371"} :step/id #uuid "3bdc3345-f3fe-56c7-9d48-45ed67552ad2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272030 :question/id #uuid "a83d9fbf-2f37-5a9f-b9ba-27fe8d23afc4"} :step/id #uuid "702b377c-b5a4-541e-aae2-92f1f1c6521f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 175921863