matrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2b6d9fc-27bd-59c7-939d-4c7647eba5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402704} {:answer/id #uuid "14f389ba-b888-572b-b782-3154d75be19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402705} {:answer/id #uuid "740563d7-e5ec-56c2-ad21-71bebf1921ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402706} {:answer/id #uuid "7e800d1f-0a48-5f64-afa7-82d01d638a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402707} {:answer/id #uuid "adfcc4ba-cd4b-5ff5-9647-ade01a07c322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402706} :answer-field/id #uuid "7901325e-ab13-5590-bad7-aa03b8ccdd1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402703}] :question/id #uuid "82160b99-37f5-52f2-85ae-e1d38b048f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-40138" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\nB\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64437bc-258f-57d6-8252-86408694ed54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404151} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fce00d-d8dc-5d30-b228-f50bd9f70fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404152} {:answer/id #uuid "ea65c7ad-6c2b-58e5-8658-7ac9d1dfed34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404153} {:answer/id #uuid "06abf95b-dbcf-5d63-a7e2-6b9ef8e25083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404154} {:answer/id #uuid "163ea62e-033a-5464-9c63-6deea7d57755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404155} :answer-field/id #uuid "630df7f3-d3b0-52b1-a138-2b8c01a3e3ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404150}] :question/id #uuid "811972db-6534-593a-aa1e-100258f3cf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81236" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\nT\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a52f5c-fb5a-567f-a05c-b4a3377da12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427851} {:answer/id #uuid "349ea5d5-1623-5b94-b22b-a5a6bd4fb201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427853} {:answer/id #uuid "65b8a476-fed9-5e7d-acb6-3aec390dd318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427857} {:answer/id #uuid "b20dbe0a-b3d3-556e-baa9-349554260ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427859} {:answer/id #uuid "f3482122-1b23-51b6-bab9-0274990d6acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427860} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef6614e3-dc36-51f8-9c16-862fd38745d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427862}] :question/id #uuid "44ed4b4e-a305-51c9-9d7d-957ba67e3cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-37605" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124381 :question/id #uuid "c1ebc3ac-7a26-565f-9798-9de531dc6e47" :question/math-academy-id "e-7133" :question/problem "Find a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n![](images/e3/e38c742a22bea651a3c410744f798297306661b6eee2929d7c5efff3bdb78844.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}= \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PDP^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order: \n\n![](images/eb/ebedba78b53f92ed5e4d69322a5e682675bdfc13b2687575fdf94f9c78a1cbe5.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/16/16933b8fd024b62ad8fafa36b382aefce74383a9bf47b952bf7e0e12fe26cae6.svg) Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0160f5a3-2ea1-5858-b954-3348e5f89bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321162} {:answer/id #uuid "02754f47-bdf7-52a7-926f-faa704f8c1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321171} {:answer/id #uuid "58f54d69-961e-58e6-8bf8-7c203ba49701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321847} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0a589e-53b0-51b6-a7aa-ab1776d86608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322006} {:answer/id #uuid "b6eff625-fb6f-5013-b9cc-cca3da5bfac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322571} :answer-field/id #uuid "5700a7c9-a32d-554d-943b-5562d53a985c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323337}] :question/id #uuid "97605f63-4e2b-5abe-a9b2-c8a3d46a4bac" :question/math-academy-id "q-37271" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}(-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 15 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e42f0896-08db-560d-a6a0-716ad1294008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -20 \\\\ 20 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122949} {:answer/id #uuid "fecbf232-6df8-57f3-9219-aca9e2329182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-25 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122950} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9b4a40-3fe1-5d0d-9850-a2209f1ef66e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122951} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2f7dc7-9e21-5e71-b60e-afd1c4183457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122952} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec212a5-da40-5312-9a85-4f50a1661f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122953}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6a623219-688e-5ead-a4e8-d6bdd81add41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122948} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afdd57a1-0170-5f78-b9f3-7d133a214662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392083} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c22fe6-7a29-5d53-bf1e-dcb01618c82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392084} {:answer/id #uuid "c648c211-0d7a-5426-9417-4c8a14e61fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-25 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392085} {:answer/id #uuid "18b5d9da-81e5-5e43-a23d-2cbb8e906e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392086} {:answer/id #uuid "dee154fe-92ae-598d-97c7-e844548af75e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -20 \\\\ 20 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392083} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b268bf-a60e-5b96-96c4-82721f1bf955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392082}] :question/id #uuid "6b5330de-42e9-59fc-85b7-be3de824baa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-37314" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $F$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $F,$ then $F$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $F=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $F,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nF={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "768e3afb-bb09-5e8d-afce-24265c595101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349537} {:answer/id #uuid "8b60cf77-97aa-5202-8d4e-811565f0e808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349708} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea6050c-f1c9-5dd1-b2f0-9331f4c3eb90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349731} {:answer/id #uuid "dd99276d-af91-5354-a572-a9a70ed58f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350384} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e335fc-583e-509e-ace9-504cc6b7c779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350384} :answer-field/id #uuid "91c9727d-1213-5c42-9fe0-34caddae9641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350954}] :question/id #uuid "c93e7214-d507-511f-96e2-a29d77beb762" :question/math-academy-id "q-81210" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 12 \\\\ 16 & 24\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7579ab44-bd79-5efe-b0c2-f284657c4cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 4 \\\\ 15 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349527} {:answer/id #uuid "8e42582e-5cb4-5323-a399-3b20a65879bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349729} {:answer/id #uuid "bb84f3e0-0aaf-50c7-8d43-3326b2bff35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350116} {:answer/id #uuid "e07ef508-ba37-54d5-9f11-5ede72a99e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}