-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346021} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ce04cad-1f03-5a00-a024-317c9a3f8ee9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347630}] :question/id #uuid "8d4333e7-3a3e-56f4-8135-82a56303935f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78253" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-60 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-60 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{1}{3}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b71045-960f-5d1c-aafb-afc7978751cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379395} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dea29e7-4ac1-55e1-9019-0bfce7fd3d88" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379394} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c03f27-805f-52aa-839d-bd4031e5bed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379397}] :a